# serverroom Provider

Manage your dedicated servers as code: their operating system, reverse DNS, uptime monitors, port-load alerts and
webhooks, and read your servers, the servers in stock and the systems each plan installs.

One provider serves every website we run. The API key decides which account, and which website's API, the provider
talks to, so there is usually nothing else to set. If the provider asks for `api_url`, set it (or
`SERVERROOM_API_URL`) to your control panel website followed by `/api`, for example `https://www.example.com/api`.

## Authentication

Create an API key in your control panel under **API & Keys** and give it only the permissions you use:

| Permission | Needed for |
|---|---|
| Account: read | `serverroom_servers`, `serverroom_server`, `serverroom_server_os` |
| Servers: read / write | `serverroom_server` addresses, `serverroom_reverse_dns`, `serverroom_server_os` reinstalls |
| Alerts: read / write | `serverroom_monitor`, `serverroom_port_alert` |
| Webhooks: read / write | `serverroom_webhook` |

`serverroom_account`, `serverroom_stock` and `serverroom_plan_os` need no permission at all.

Pass the key as the `SERVERROOM_API_KEY` environment variable rather than writing it in a file.

An API key can never order, pay for or cancel a service, so servers are referenced by their service id (the number
in your control panel and in `data.serverroom_servers`), not created. `data.serverroom_stock` gives each machine in
stock with its order link.

